    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §494, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7211\n\nSPONSOR: FELDER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act the amend the general business law, in relation to denial of\naccess to an employee toilet facility\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill seeks to clarify that state and local consumer protection\ndepartments have the power and duty to handle violations of the Crohn's\nand Colitis Fairness Act.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the general business law by adding a new section 494 to\nprovide that the state and local consumer protection bureaus shall have\nthe power and duty to receive complaints from any individual that has\nbeen denied access to an employee toilet facility in violation of this\narticle; to attempt to mediate such complaints where appropriate; and to\nrefer complaints to the appropriate unit of the department or the feder-\nal, state or other agency authorized by law for appropriate action on\nsuch complaints.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nChapter 471 of 2017 established the Crohn's and Colitis Fairness Act to\npermit individuals with certain medical conditions access to employee\ntoilet facilities in places of business when necessary. In his approval\nmessage for this bill, Governor Cuomo noted that \"individuals subject to\nthe protections of this law may file a complaint by contacting the\nconsumer protection bureau of their local or state government, including\nthose located within the New York State Department of State or the New\nYork Attorney General's Office.\"  However, some consumer protection\ndepartments have not been enforcing the law since this authority was\nonly included in the Governor's message and not in the text of the law.\n\nThe bill adds a new section to the Crohn's and Colitis Fairness Act to\nprovide that the state and local consumer protection departments shall\nhave the power and duty to receive complaints from anyone that has been\ndenied access to an employee toilet facility in violation of the Act; to\nattempt to mediate such complaints where appropriate; and to refer\ncomplaints to the appropriate unit of the department or the federal,\nstate or other agency authorized by law for appropriate action on such\ncomplaints.\n\nCodifying into law that the consumer protection departments have\nenforcement authority will help to better inform impacted individuals\nseeking redress and will also lead to more expeditious handling and\nresolution of violations.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
